{
  "name": "First Frame to Video - MiniMax H3",
  "author": "InvokeAI",
  "description": "First-frame-to-video generation with MiniMax H3 (FL2VA): the video starts from your image and animates it per the prompt, with a jointly generated AAC stereo soundtrack muxed into the MP4. The canvas is derived automatically from the image's aspect ratio (768px short edge, max 768x1344).",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "contact": "",
  "tags": "minimax h3, image to video, first frame, video, audio",
  "notes": "This workflow needs the MiniMax H3 FL2VA model (diffusers layout). Each InvokeAI install assigns its own internal model IDs, so the model field is left blank -- select your installed MiniMax H3 model in the form after loading.\n\nMiniMax H3 generates video and a synchronized stereo soundtrack together; the soundtrack is muxed into the MP4 automatically (AAC, 32 kHz). The frame rate is fixed at 24 fps and frame counts snap to a 17n+5 grid: 124 frames = ~5.2 s, up to 345 frames = 14.4 s. Width/height must be multiples of 32; the native canvas is a 768 px short edge (max 768x1344).\n\nThe model is guidance-distilled: no negative prompt, no CFG. Steps counts sigma grid points; 50 is the reference default.\n\nThe first frame is part of BOTH the text conditioning (vision context) and the VAE frame conditioning -- the denoise node rejects the graph if they diverge. This workflow wires the one First Frame image node and the shared Width/Height nodes to every consumer, so the coupling holds automatically; keep it that way if you edit a copy. The first frame is stretched onto the target canvas: pick Width/Height to match its aspect ratio.",
